  <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <div type="mūlam" rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="TNV1">
      <l>akattatum puṟattatu māyiru pakutiyiṉ</l>
<l>mikuttatu māki virintatu poruḷē.</l></lg>
<lg xml:lang="en">
  <l><term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term><note xml:lang="en" place="foot">I leave such technical terms untranslated in the <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> in which they are defined. 
If pushed, the oft-given translations of ‘poetic content’ or ‘poetics’ come pretty close. The nature of a theoretical text such as this is that terms are constantly being defined. </note> is expanded<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">‘Expanded’ here in the sense of elaborated upon or explained. Defining something expands it, in a way, using multiple words to explain one word. Another interpretation would be to understand that the author is expanding the scope of <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term>, incorporating a third category.</note> into that which is <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akam</hi></term>,
that which is <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṟam</hi></term>,</l> 
<l>and that which exceeds those two categories.</l>
</lg>
</div>
</body>
</text>
<text xml:lang="ta" type="edition" corresp="#Ed">
    <body>
      <head xml:lang="en">Commentary</head>
      <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV1comm1">eṉpatu niṟutta muṟaiyāṉē poruḷ ilakkaṇam ām āṟuṇarttutaṉutaliṟṟu. eṉavē yivvōttup poruḷ iyal eṉpatāyiṟṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en">The quoted [<term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term>] intends to make known the 
way in which the grammar of poetic content occurs in accordance with established tradition.<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">This common phrase, <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">niṟutta muṟaiyāṉē</hi></q>, either refers to a more abstract concept of a scholastic or intellectual tradition, or to an order of enumeration (see the commentary to TNV 5). Here both readings are plausible. The ‘established tradition’ in question may refer to a specific school of thought, or it may be an empty reference, situating this text in the continuum of non-specific ‘traditional’ thought. Were this to refer to a place within an enumeration, it could mean that this chapter is occupying its rightful place in the traditional enumeration of the divisions of grammar. What exactly those divisions are depends on which tradition we are talking about. The <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">Tolkāppiyam</foreign> conceptualises three fields of grammar – <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">eḻuttu</foreign> (‘phonology’), <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">col</foreign> (‘morpho-syntax’), and <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">poruḷ</foreign> (‘poetics’). One can add a fourth division with <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">yāppu</foreign> (‘metrics’) and a fifth with <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">aṇi</foreign> (‘poetic ornamentation’). UVC cites this sentence in his introduction as evidence that the TNV had multiple books, of which all but this one have now been lost, so one could conclude that he understood the second option (UVC 1937, vi, quoted and translated in \S\ref{sec:lost-sections}).</note> This chapter is therefore called ‘the nature of <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term>’.</p>
</div>
<div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV1comm2">akattiṉ ākiya vakap poruḷum puṟattiṉ ākiya
puṟap poruḷum āy iru vakaiyiṉum ākiya vakap puṟap poruḷum eṉa mū vīṟṟatāyp pal vēṟu
vakaip paṭṭa tuṟaikaḷāṟ parappuṭaiyatu poruḷ ām eṉṟa vāṟu.</p>
<p xml:lang="en"><term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akapporuḷ</hi></term> that is about the interior;
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">puṟapporuḷ</hi></term> that is about the exterior; and
  <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">akappuṟapporuḷ</hi></term> that is about both of those categories – 
  being [thus] divided into three [categories], <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">poruḷ</hi></term> possesses a breadth [of content] by means of poetic situations of many different types.</p>
</div>
  <div type="commentary" rend="parallel">
<p xml:id="TNV1comm3">puṟac ceykai yuḷateṉiṉumikuti vakaiyāl akam paṟṟi nikaḻtaliṉ
iru talaiyum tīrā vaṉpiṟṟākiya kāmam<anchor xml:id="kamam"/> akap poruḷ eṉpatu. akanikaḻcci yuḷateṉiṉumikuti vakaiyāṟ puṟac 
ceykaiyāṉikaḻtaliṟ kuṟṟappāṭillā maṟam puṟap poruḷ eṉpatu.<anchor xml:id="enpatu"/> akam allā viṉpamum puṟam allāp poruḷ ceyal 
vakaiyum aṟamum ataṉ payaṉ ākiya vīṭum iru vakai nikaḻcciyunōkkik<anchor xml:id="nokki"/> kōṭaliṉ akap puṟap poruḷ eṉap paṭṭaṉa veṉpatu. <num>1.</num></p>
<p xml:lang="en">Even though there is external action, because of it occurring by way of the interior predominantly,<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The phrase <q xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">mikuti vakaiyāl</hi></q> denotes that it is by means of the category of abundance or excess that a conclusion is reached – <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">vakai</foreign> is the criterion of classification. In this case, it is because the poetic content predominantly treats interior themes that a poem is considered <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign>.</note> the desire<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">My inclination is that <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kāmam</hi></term> must refer to something slightly different from <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">aṉpu</hi></term> – ‘love’. In the context of this text, where we will see the initial stage of the relationship referred to as <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">teyvappuṇarcci</hi></term> (‘divine union’) in <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">cūttiram</hi></term> 15, the author conceives of the relationship as being either divinely ordained – fated, in other words – or is trying to gloss over the sexual implication in the earlier terminology – <term xml:lang="ta-Latn"><hi rend="italic">kāmappuṇarcci</hi></term> (‘union based on desire’, found in TP 487i, IA 2). The commentary brings in some of the connotations of the earlier term here while incorporating the ‘divine’ notion with the characterisation of their desire as one of ‘undying love’. I take this to refer to the fact that they had been in love with each other in their previous lives, and that their relationship in this life is now preordained (Zvelebil 1986, 3).</note> that is the undying love of the two principal [characters] is called <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akapporuḷ</foreign>. And even though there is the occurrence of interior [content], because of it occurring by way of external action predominantly, faultless valour is called <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">puṟapporuḷ</foreign>. Pleasure that is not <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign>, the type of wealth-making that is not <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">puṟam</foreign>, virtue, and the liberation that is their result<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The syntactic relationship between liberation and the preceding <foreign xml:lang="sa-Latn">puruṣārtha</foreign>-s is ambiguous. It could either be the result of the three preceding elements, or one can connect it to virtue alone: ‘Pleasure… wealth, virtue, and the liberation that is its fruit.’ I connect it with all three elements as I believe this is an oblique reference to didactic literature, specifically the <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">Tirukkuṟaḷ</foreign>, which treats non-<foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign> pleasure, non-<foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">puṟam</foreign> worldly pursuits, and virtue in its three books. Living in accordance with the tenets outlined regarding those three aspects of life leads to liberation. This supports the claim in \S\ref{sec:akappuram} of the introduction to this edition that <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akappuṟam</foreign> was conceptualised so the grammar of poetics could cover didactic literature, which was absent from the Caṅkam corpus.</note> – because of looking at and understanding the occurrence of both categories [of poetry],<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">The categories in question are the two hypergenres: <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign> and <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">puṟam</foreign>. As I understand it, this refers to didactic poems that contain both <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akam</foreign> and <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">puṟam</foreign> elements.</note> they are called <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">akappuṟapporuḷ</foreign>.</p></div>
 </body>
 </text>     
